{
 "lead": "A travelling wave moves energy, not matter. Each small piece of the string swings with the same amplitude and frequency, and hands its energy to the next piece. That is how a plucked guitar string pushes air, and how an antenna sends power outward. This chapter gives the formula for that power, then watches two equal waves travelling in opposite directions. They superpose into a standing wave: a pattern that oscillates in place, with dead points called nodes that no energy ever crosses.",
 "prereq": "You need the wave equation and the string speed v = √(T/μ) from <a href=\"#wave-equation-and-waves-on-a-stretched-string\">2.4</a>, and the energy of SHM from <a href=\"#simple-harmonic-motion\">2.1</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Energy and power of a travelling wave",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "On a stretched string, every small element of length dx oscillates in simple harmonic motion. Its mass is μ dx, where μ is the mass per unit length in kg/m. For SHM the energy of a mass m with amplitude A and angular frequency ω = 2πf is ½ m ω² A². Putting m = μ dx, the energy in that element is ½ μ ω² A² dx. So the energy per unit length is u = ½ μ ω² A², in J/m."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In a time dt a fresh length v dt of string starts moving, where v is the wave speed. Power is energy per unit time, so P = u v = ½ μ v ω² A²."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P = ½ μ v ω² A²",
     "where": "P is the average power in W; μ is the mass per unit length in kg/m; v is the wave speed in m/s; ω = 2πf is the angular frequency in rad/s; A is the amplitude in m."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Notice the squares. Double the amplitude and the power becomes four times larger. Double the frequency and again it is four times larger. For a wave spreading in a three-dimensional medium the same argument gives intensity I = ½ ρ v ω² A² in W/m², where ρ is the density in kg/m³ and I is power per unit area. Only a few universities teach this derivation at this depth; the result is what matters."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Two opposite waves make a standing wave",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Send two waves of the same amplitude and frequency in opposite directions: y₁ = A sin(kx − ωt) and y₂ = A sin(kx + ωt). Here k = 2π/λ is the wave number in rad/m. Adding them with sin(C + D) + sin(C − D) = 2 sin C cos D, where C = kx and D = ωt, gives y = 2A sin(kx) cos(ωt)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The x part and the t part have separated. Each point of the string now just oscillates in time, with amplitude 2A|sin kx|. Where sin kx = 0 the amplitude is zero: these are nodes, at x = 0, λ/2, λ, … and they never move. Halfway between nodes |sin kx| = 1: these are antinodes, swinging through 2A. No energy travels along the string; it shifts back and forth between kinetic and elastic form, and never crosses a node."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A string fixed at both ends must have a node at x = 0 and at x = L, so L must hold a whole number of half wavelengths: L = nλ/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "λₙ = 2L/n,   fₙ = n v/(2L),   n = 1, 2, 3, …",
     "where": "λₙ is the wavelength of the nth mode in m; L is the length between the fixed ends in m; fₙ is its frequency in Hz; v is the wave speed in m/s; n = 1 gives the fundamental."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "So f₁ = v/(2L) and the higher modes are 2f₁, 3f₁, … Only this ladder of frequencies can exist on the string. That is why a guitar string sounds a musical note and not a hiss."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
